## Step 2: copy the remaining 17 (only after step 1 passes)

The script is safe to re-run, because it skips secrets that already exist, and it prints only names and a result:
```bash
P=pg-ae-n-app-125609; L=me-central2
KEYS="DB_HOST DB_USERNAME DB_PASSWORD DB_DATABASE DB_PORT GCP_BUCKET GCP_FILE_PATH MAIL_DRIVER MAIL_HOST MAIL_PORT MAIL_USERNAME MAIL_PASSWORD MAIL_FROM_NAME MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS REDIS_HOST REDIS_PORT REDIS_PASSWORD EMAIL_SENDING_ENABLED"
for k in $KEYS; do
  s=CN_STAGING_$k
  if ! gcloud secrets describe $s --location=$L --project=$P >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    gcloud secrets create $s --location=$L --project=$P >/dev/null || { echo "CREATE FAILED $s"; continue; }
    gcloud secrets versions access latest --secret=$s --project=$P | gcloud secrets versions add $s --location=$L --project=$P --data-file=- >/dev/null
  fi
  a=$(gcloud secrets versions access latest --secret=$s --project=$P | sha256sum)
  b=$(gcloud secrets versions access latest --secret=$s --location=$L --project=$P | sha256sum)
  [ "$a" = "$b" ] && echo "OK $s" || echo "MISMATCH $s"
done
```
All 18 lines should say `OK`. Nothing live changes yet, because the running app still reads the global secrets.

## Step 3: switch the app, in the backend repo

In `ecosystem.config.js`, add one line to `env_staging`:
```js
      env_staging: {
        NODE_ENV: 'staging',
        PORT: 3024,
        SECRET_LOCATION: 'me-central2',
      },
```
Push to `staging`. **Packer is the safety test:** the build VM starts the app with this setting, and the pipeline fails at the `/health` check if the regional secrets can't be read, before any server is replaced. Then the rolling update replaces the running server.

## Step 4: remove the global copies, only after days of stable running

⚠️ Deleting a secret can't be undone. Until then, rollback is removing that line and pushing. After deleting, the old global values are gone. Before deleting, disable the global versions first as a reversible trial, and watch for a successful new server boot.

